## Why I made these choices, and what you should change

**Level of detail:** This is roughly the right depth. It's specific enough to act as a foundation for every future chapter, but it's people-and-constraints focused rather than exhaustive. Notice what I *didn't* include — no full asset lists (that's Chapter 02), no data classification (Chapter 03), no compliance detail (Chapter 04). Each chapter should answer one question well; the profile answers "who is this organization and what are its constraints?"

**The "Security-Relevant Facts" section is the most important part.** Every constraint listed there should get echoed later — e.g., tiny security team shows up in your risk assessment and gap analysis as justification for control choices. This is what makes the project feel like real-world risk reasoning rather than academic exercises.

**Named personnel with personalities are your friend.** "Okafor functions as de facto CISO" gives every later policy a real author and every incident case study real characters. Feel free to rename everyone — make the names your own. Actually, **you should rename all of them** — these are just my inventions, and it'll feel more authentically yours.

**Things to personalize or verify:**
1. **Rename Cetara... I mean**, rename the bank if you want — "Meridian" is just a suggestion; make sure it's not a real bank in those markets (watch out: there are banks with "Meridian" in their name in the mid-Atlantic — a quick search of the FDIC BankFind tool will tell you if you care; being fictional-but-named-after-a-real-bank is awkward)
2. Adjust the Fiserv choice if you prefer Jack Henry or Fis — all plausible for this size bank
3. The NY DFS "CISO designation" detail is real regulation applied realistically — verify it when you write Chapter 04 so your story stays consistent
4. Fix the mermaid code block — GitHub renders `mermaid` fenced blocks natively, so this org chart and your future network diagrams will just work

**One honest caution:** don't let this read as AI-written in its final form. Use this as your scaffold — reword sections into your own voice as you go through it. The *thinking* (constraints, structure, trade-offs) is the value; the sentences should sound like you wrote them, because in interviews you'll be asked about this company in detail.

